Block pointers above 16383: a bank in TEX1, 16-bit BITBLTBUF pointers

TEX0's TBP0 and CBP are 14 bits each and every bit of TEX0 is a field, so a
block at 16384 or more (GS memory beyond 4 MB, GSOptions::vram_size) cannot be
named by TEX0. The high bits ride in TEX1's undefined bits: TBP0's in bits
48-51 (TBP0_BANK), CBP's in bits 52-55 (CBP_BANK). BITBLTBUF's SBP and DBP
become 16 bits (they were 14 followed by two spare bits). A mip level's 14-bit
pointer is read as a distance from the base picture's pointer, modulo 16384
(tex_level_tbp): levels follow their base in memory and span less than 16384
blocks, so a level needs no bank of its own, and a picture may straddle a
bank boundary.

Every place that read TBP0, CBP, SBP or DBP as the block now reads the full
pointer: the texture descriptor (its TEX1 bits, hashed, keep the TBP0 bank;
the CBP bank is cleared with CBP), the page rectangles of a texture and of a
palette, the feedback tests, the cached CLUT pointers (CLD compares), the
forwarded CLUT, the transfer tracking, the texture upload's mip table, the
CLUT descriptor, and the texture listener (on_clut_load takes the palette's
full pointer). TEX2 changes only the CLUT fields and keeps the banks.

A TEX1 write that changes the sampling only (the game's filter change, written
between binding a texture and drawing it) sets bit 56, KEEP_BANK: it keeps the
banks of the TEX1 it replaces and the bit is not stored. Without it the write
would clear the bank of the texture bound.

For a block below 16384 every bank bit is zero and nothing changes.

GSOptions::ignore_bank (a control for tests) reads TEX1's bank bits as zero.

